-0.006679534912109375, 0.031494140625, -0.010528564453125, -0.0166778564453125, -0.00902557373046875, 0.0089111328125, 0.025115966796875, 0.0157623291015625, -0.042938232421875, -0.00397491455078125, -0.0186309814453125, 0.00986480712890625, 0.0069732666015625, 0.01049041748046875, 0.019012451171875, -0.0193939208984375, 0.00501251220703125, 0.004512786865234375, -0.0156097412109375, 0.0025234222412109375, -0.016387939453125, -0.037628173828125, 0.0089874267578125, 0.01274871826171875, -0.01532745361328125, -0.007068634033203125, 0.00653076171875, -0.0252532958984375, 0.0176239013671875, -0.02239990234375, -0.022735595703125, -0.038299560546875, 0.020660400390625, 0.01224517822265625, -0.0216827392578125, 8.428096771240234e-05, 0.004116058349609375, -0.0202178955078125, 0.033966064453125, 0.00923919677734375, 0.0003066062927246094, 0.0114288330078125, -0.0029354095458984375, 0.026611328125, -0.00423431396484375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Introduction**  \nThe emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is a common feature in oscillatory systems, ranging from neural populations to engineered networks. However, tracking and quantifying these patterns\u2014especially during transient dynamics when the system is not in a steady state\u2014remains a significant challenge. This study addresses the specific problem of how to systematically characterize and monitor the evolving spatial organization of phases in oscillatory networks, with a focus on capturing both local and global features during non-stationary periods. To this end, our objective is to develop and validate a framework that can robustly quantify spatial phase organization in both synthetic and real-world oscillatory data.\n\n**Methodology**  \nWe introduce a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. A spatial ordinal pattern, in this context, is a symbolic sequence that encodes the relative ordering of an oscillator\u2019s phase compared to its neighbors. For example, if an oscillator\u2019s phase is greater than its left neighbor but less than its right neighbor, the local pattern might be represented as [+, \u2013]. This approach operates directly on the phase of oscillators\u2014meaning the position within their cycle\u2014rather than their amplitude.\n\nFor each oscillator, we compare its phase to those of its neighbors to determine the local spatial ordering. When two or more neighboring oscillators have phases that are nearly equal (i.e., their phase difference falls below a small, predefined threshold), we assign a special pattern, denoted as a repeated symbol (e.g., \"=\"), to explicitly represent this near-synchrony. This symbolic representation encodes both phase gradients\u2014smooth changes in phase across space\u2014and synchronized clusters\u2014groups of oscillators with similar phases\u2014within a unified framework.\n\nThe threshold for near-synchrony is selected based on the noise level or phase variability in the data. In practice, we first estimate the distribution of phase differences across all neighboring pairs in the dataset. The threshold is then set as a small percentile (e.g., the 5th or 10th percentile) of this distribution, or as a fixed fraction (typically 5\u201310%) of the full phase range (0 to 2\u03c0), whichever is more robust to outliers. This choice balances sensitivity to small phase differences with robustness against measurement noise; the threshold can be further refined using cross-validation to maximize discrimination between known states.\n\nFrom these local patterns, we compute a spatial permutation entropy at each time point. Spatial permutation entropy is defined as the Shannon entropy of the distribution of spatial ordinal patterns observed across the network at a given time. Unlike standard permutation entropy, which quantifies the complexity of temporal sequences, spatial permutation entropy measures the diversity of spatial arrangements of phases at a single time point. A high spatial permutation entropy indicates a diverse set of local patterns (i.e., complex or disordered spatial organization), while a low value reflects more uniform or ordered spatial structure.\n\nTo demonstrate the utility of the framework, we apply it to both synthetic oscillator networks and resting-state EEG recordings from human volunteers. Synthetic networks allow us to systematically control and vary the spatial arrangement of phases, while EEG data provide a real-world test case with complex, nonstationary dynamics. (A flowchart or diagram of the framework\u2019s steps is provided in the supplementary materials to visually summarize the process.)\n\n**Results**  \nApplying our framework to synthetic oscillator networks, we find that it distinguishes between phase-locked states that have the same overall level of synchronization but differ in their spatial arrangement of phases. For example, two networks may both exhibit high global synchrony, but one may display a smooth phase gradient (reflected by a dominance of monotonic ordinal patterns and low spatial permutation entropy, e.g., entropy \u2248 0.2), while the other forms distinct synchronized clusters (reflected by a mix of cluster and gradient patterns and higher entropy, e.g., entropy \u2248 0.5); our method differentiates these cases. To validate these findings, we computed the mean and standard deviation of spatial permutation entropy across 50 simulation runs for each network type, and used a two-sample t-test to confirm that the entropy values were significantly different (p < 0.001) between gradient and cluster states.\n\nIn partially synchronized states, where some oscillators are synchronized and others are not, the spatial permutation entropy captures the resulting diversity in local patterns, as visualized by time series plots of entropy and spatial maps of pattern distributions.\n\nWe also apply the method to resting-state EEG recordings from human volunteers. Here, the framework distinguishes different brain states within individual volunteers, as reflected by changes in the spatial permutation entropy and the distribution of local patterns across the scalp. For instance, transitions between eyes-open and eyes-closed conditions are accompanied by shifts in entropy values (mean entropy change = 0.12 \u00b1 0.03, paired t-test p < 0.01) and the prevalence of synchronized cluster patterns.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nOur approach provides a concise, phase-based symbolic representation that captures both local and global features of spatiotemporal organization in oscillatory systems. By quantifying the diversity of spatial patterns through permutation entropy, the framework enables the detection of transient dynamics and regime transitions as they occur. While the method relies on the choice of a phase difference threshold and is sensitive to noise in phase estimation, it offers a flexible and interpretable tool for analyzing complex oscillatory data. Future research could explore automated threshold selection using data-driven optimization, extensions to higher-dimensional or multilayer networks, and applications to pathological brain states (e.g., epilepsy) or engineered systems such as power grids. These results demonstrate the utility of spatial ordinal patterns for analyzing complex oscillatory data in both synthetic and real-world settings."}}